  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 300 AM EDT Wed Sep 16 2026\n\nGOES-East Nighttime Microphysics RGB imagery around midnight \nEastern shows clear skies across much of the UP except for some \nhigh clouds along the Lake Michigan shores associated with \nmoisture transport around a Southeast US ridge with disturbed \nweather about the periphery of this ridge remaining well to the \nsouth of the UP. Elsewhere around North America, 500mb charts \nshow the persistent Hudson Bay trough but otherwise mostly zonal\nflow across the CONUS and Southern Canada with the exception of\na pronounced trough over the Pacific Northwest. For the rest of\nthe overnight period, some patches of fog cannot be ruled out, \nbut HREF probabilities of visibility falling below 1 mile are \nonly 10-33%. While the setup supports effective radiational \ncooling, widespread dew points in the 40s and low 50s will keep \nlow temperatures in the 40s for most this morning, though a few \ntypical dry/cold spots in the interior west may see temperatures\nfalling into the upper 30s with chances of frost on elevated \nsurfaces at around 10-20%.\n\nAs surface high pressure moves directly over the region today, \nexpect light winds with Great Lakes lake breezes in the afternoon \nand highs in the 60s to low 70s. Meanwhile, a shortwave will move \nfrom northern Saskatchewan to far northwestern Ontario throughout \ntoday and tonight. This will bring with it a weak surface boundary \nand chances of scattered rain showers beginning a bit before sunset \nin the west and spreading east overnight. Rain will not be \nwidespread - HREF probabilities of measurable rainfall tonight are \nas high as 50-75% along the MI/WI state line and north of Houghton, \nbut between Houghton and the US-41/M-28 corridor, chances are around \n15%. Accumulations are not expected to be particularly impactful \neither, as the 75th percentile of precip totals only exceed 0.1 \ninches for IMT/ESC and south. The precipitation will contribute to \nadded low-level moisture overnight, which will bring back ~20% \nchances of fog overnight into Thursday morning.\n\nRidging in the wake of that shortwave will bring a return of dry \nweather for the remainder of Thursday into Friday morning with light \nwinds mainly out of the north. Expect highs around the 70s degree \nmark with lows into Friday morning settling into the 40s away from \nthe Great Lakes. The next chances at rain arrive Friday and Saturday \nwith another shortwave, though the current NBM keeps PoPs above 35% \nconfined to the southern half of the UP and each of the last 3 runs \nhas trended slightly south with the track of precipitation. Current \nLREF chances of half an inch of rainfall over a 24 hour period are \nonly in excess of 50% for the southern half of Menominee County this \nweekend, with chances of similar rainfall as far north as the US-2 \ncorridor around 30%. Model spread and forecast uncertainty \ngrows significantly beyond the weekend as ensembles struggle to \nagree on how the Pacific Northwest trough progresses through the\nnorthern CONUS.",
